course: adult-life
lesson: 27
title: Coffee, tea, wine and the pub
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many grown-ups drink things that change how they feel: coffee and tea wake them up, and alcohol, in wine, beer and spirits, relaxes them and in larger amounts makes them clumsy and silly.
explain: Most adults drink alcohol in small amounts with food or with friends, which is legal and usually harmless; drinking too much causes headaches, accidents and, for some people, a real illness.
explain: Picture a wedding in Galway: Auntie Siobhan giggles that she is "a bit tipsy", Uncle Pat says he'll "just have the one" and has three, and the next morning Dad lies in a dark room asking everyone to whisper.
explain: Alcohol is against the law to buy before 18 because growing bodies and brains handle it badly.
explain: When a grown-up drinks too much, it is never the child's fault and never the child's job to fix.
fact: Coffee contains caffeine, which makes the brain feel more awake; people who drink it every morning feel slow and grumpy without it.
fact: Too much caffeine can make people jittery, give them a fast heartbeat and stop them sleeping well, and then they drink more coffee because they are tired.
fact: In Britain and Ireland "let's put the kettle on" means "let's stop, sit down and deal with this together"; the pause matters more than the tea.
fact: Wine is a drink with alcohol in it; one glass with a meal makes many adults feel a little relaxed and is a normal, legal grown-up treat.
fact: A pub is a place where adults meet, talk, sometimes eat and usually drink beer; time flies when people chat, so "an hour" often becomes several.
fact: "I'll just have the one" means a person plans to drink only one alcoholic drink, often because they are driving; it is a well-known joke because one so often becomes three.
fact: Drinking and driving is against the law and dangerous.
fact: Tipsy means a little alcohol has made someone chattier and sillier than usual, though they are still in control.
fact: Drunk means so much alcohol that balance, speech and judgement stop working properly; drunk people can fall, fight and act unpredictably.
fact: A hangover is how the body feels the day after drinking too much alcohol: headache, tiredness, thirst and feeling sick.
fact: Dry January is when people choose to drink no alcohol for the whole month, to feel healthier, save money and check that drinking is a choice, not a need.
fact: Alcoholism, or alcohol addiction, is an illness in which a person needs alcohol so badly they cannot stop even when it hurts them and their family; people can get better with help.
fact: "I need a drink" usually just means "I've had a hard day", but using alcohol every time things go wrong can turn into a hard habit.
fact: If someone in your family drinks too much, it is never your fault; tell a trusted adult or call a children's helpline such as Childline (0800 1111 in the UK).
exercise: Dad at 7 a.m.: "Don't talk to me before my coffee." What does he mean? | He feels slow and grumpy until his coffee wakes him up | He is angry with you for something you did yesterday and is still sulking | Coffee makes him unable to hear | He is not allowed to talk before breakfast
exercise: What is in coffee that makes people feel more awake? | Caffeine | Sugar | Alcohol | Vitamins
exercise: Mum to a friend: "I'm on my fourth coffee and it's only eleven." What is she really saying? | She is very tired or very busy | She is thirsty because it is hot | She has won a coffee competition | She is trying to give up coffee
exercise: What can too much coffee do to a grown-up? | Make them jittery and stop them sleeping well | Make them fall asleep straight away and sleep all night | Make them grow taller | Make them drunk and dizzy
exercise: Gran, whenever anything happens: "Let's put the kettle on." What does she really mean? | Let's stop, sit down and deal with this together | She is very thirsty and needs a drink of something hot | The kettle is broken and needs fixing | It is time for everyone to go home
exercise: Mum and Dad have a glass of wine with Friday dinner. What is that? | A normal, small grown-up treat | A sign they have a drink problem | Something against the law at home | Medicine for a cold
exercise: Why are children not allowed to buy alcohol? | Young bodies and brains handle it badly | It tastes far too sweet and sticky for children's teeth | Children would spend all their money | Children cannot open the bottles
exercise: "We'll pop to the pub for an hour" — they come back after four hours. Why? | Chatting with friends makes time fly | The pub locked them in for the evening | The clocks in pubs run slowly | They had to work in the pub
exercise: Uncle at the barbecue: "I'll just have the one." What does he mean? | He plans to drink only one alcoholic drink | He only wants one burger and then he will go | He will stay for one hour | He has only one pound left
exercise: Why might a grown-up have only one drink? | They are driving or have work tomorrow | One drink is the legal limit for a week | Two drinks are poisonous | The second drink is always more expensive
exercise: Auntie giggling at a wedding: "I'm a bit tipsy!" What does that mean? | A little alcohol has made her silly but she is in control | She has hurt her foot dancing and keeps wobbling about | She is very drunk and cannot stand up at all on her own | She is dizzy from spinning around
exercise: A man is shouting, swaying and falling over in the street at night. What is most likely? | He is very drunk | He is practising a dance | He is a tipsy wedding guest | He is cold and shivering
exercise: Dad on Sunday morning, curtains shut: "Keep the noise down, I've got a headache." What probably caused it? | Drinking too much alcohol the night before | Eating far too many green vegetables at dinner last night | Sleeping too long in the dark | Catching a cold from you
exercise: Mum in January: "I'm doing Dry January." What is she doing? | Not drinking alcohol for the whole month | Not having a bath for the whole month | Drinking only water for the whole month | Not watering the garden for the whole month
exercise: A grown-up whispers: "He has a problem with drink." What do they mean? | He is addicted to alcohol and cannot stop | He does not like the taste of plain water from the tap | He spills his drinks a lot | He drinks too slowly at meals
exercise: Is alcohol addiction just a lack of willpower? | No, it is an illness that people can get help for | Yes, they could stop any time they liked if they tried harder | Yes, it only happens to bad people | No, it is caused by drinking coffee
exercise: A friend's dad after a hard day: "I need a drink." What does he usually mean? | He has had a stressful day and wants to relax | He is extremely thirsty after running | He is addicted and cannot stop | He wants you to fetch him water
exercise: If someone in your family drinks too much, whose fault is it? | Never the child's fault | The child's fault for being noisy | The child's fault for not hiding it | Nobody's, so tell no one
test: Your sister says "Dad's grumpy before coffee 'cause he hates us." Put her right. | No, he is just slow and sleepy until the caffeine works | Yes, coffee is how he stops hating us | Yes, he only likes us after lunch | No, he is grumpy because the coffee is always far too hot to drink
test: At 11 p.m. Mum is wide awake, buzzing and moaning she can't sleep. She had five coffees. What is the likely link? | Too much caffeine is keeping her awake | Coffee always makes people sleep at once | Her bed is too soft for her | She is worried about the coffee price
test: Bad news on the phone; Gran hangs up and says "right, kettle on." Why? | Making tea is her way of pausing and coping together | She is far too thirsty to think about the news until she has had a drink | She is pretending nothing happened | The news was about her kettle
test: Uncle Dave at the barbecue: "Just the one, I'm driving." Two hours later he's on his third beer and reaching for the car keys. What is right? | It is unsafe; ask to go home with someone sober | It is fine, he said he would have only the one pint | It is fine as long as he drives slowly and carefully | It is fine because he is a grown-up
test: Overheard at a wedding: "Look at Shona, she's well merry, dancing on the chairs." What does "merry" mean here? | Tipsy from drinking alcohol | Very angry with the band | Feeling sick and tired | Getting married herself
test: Dad, groaning: "Never again. Why did I let Mick buy that last round?" What is Dad suffering from? | A hangover from drinking too much | A cold he caught from Mick | Tiredness from working late | Toothache from too many sweets
test: Ellie: "Mum's doing Dry January so she must be an alcoholic." Is Ellie right? | No, many people do it to feel healthier and take a break | Yes, only alcoholics stop drinking for a month, everyone else carries on | Yes, the doctor makes alcoholics do it | No, it is a month when you cannot drink water
test: A man outside the chippy is yelling, swaying and can hardly stand. Mum pulls you closer. What is sensible? | Stay with your grown-up and keep your distance | Go up to him politely and ask him to stop shouting | Laugh at him and copy how he walks | Offer to help him walk home alone
test: Liam whispers: "My dad drinks every night and gets scary. I think it's my fault cos I'm loud." What is true? | It is not his fault, and he should tell a trusted adult | It is his fault, so he should try harder to be quieter at home | It is a secret, so he must tell no one | He should hide all the bottles himself
test: Mum on the phone: "Honestly, after the week I've had, I need a drink." What does she most likely mean? | She has had a hard week and wants to relax | She has not had any water all week | She has an alcohol addiction | She wants to go out and get drunk tonight
test: Which is a normal, everyday way grown-ups drink alcohol? | A glass of wine with Sunday dinner | A bottle of whisky before work | Drinking alone in the car | Drinking every time something goes wrong
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the sale of alcoholic beverages to persons under eighteen years of age is prohibited. | The developing body and brain are harmed by alcohol | Alcoholic beverages are excessively expensive for minors | Minors are unable to open the containers | The flavour is unsuitable for younger palates
exam: It is given that an individual states an intention to consume "just the one" drink on account of driving subsequently. Determine the principal reason for this limit. | Driving after drinking alcohol is unlawful and dangerous | A single drink is the maximum served at a barbecue | Drivers are obliged to consume one drink before departure | Alcohol improves the performance of a motor vehicle
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises a hangover. | Unpleasant bodily effects on the day following excessive drinking | A pleasant sensation experienced during the consumption of fine wine | An illness contracted from another person at a party | A condition caused by the consumption of coffee
exam: Ascertain which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning alcohol addiction. | It is an illness from which a person may recover with assistance | It is a deliberate choice which the person may cease at any moment at will | It is invariably the fault of the person's children | It affects only persons who drink wine with meals
